<p>
A couple of months ago, I wrote about <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=13571">friction in saving files</a>. Emacs, of course, tries to protect you from unsaved buffers by periodically backing them up to a special file. Recovering data from that file can be a bit finicky and since Emacs and modern computers don&rsquo;t crash very often, you don&rsquo;t get a lot of practice.
</p>
<p>
A long time ago, Bozhidar Batsov solved that problem with <a href="https://github.com/bbatsov/super-save/">super-save</a> that saved buffers whenever the buffer loses focus or perhaps every few seconds. It&rsquo;s all configurable, of course. In the mean time, James Cherti wrote <a href="https://www.jamescherti.com/buffer-guardian-el-automatically-save-emacs-buffers/">buffer-guardian.el that does pretty much the same thing</a>.
</p>
<p>
Cherti&rsquo;s solution took advantage of more recent Emacs features to provide a more reliable solution. When Batsov &ldquo;stumbled upon buffer-guardian.el&rdquo; he decided to revisit super-saver, which he hadn&rsquo;t touched since 2023. He&rsquo;s improved the detection of buffer focus change no matter how it&rsquo;s invoked. There are a several other improvements that you can read about in <a href="https://github.com/bbatsov/super-save/">Batsov&rsquo;s post</a>.
</p>
<p>
I&rsquo;m inclined not to like my software doing things automatically that I&rsquo;m used to asking for explicitly so neither buffer-guardian nor super-saver appeal to me but I&rsquo;m probably in the minority on this so many of you probably <i>will</i> like using one of these packages. As I say, I don&rsquo;t use either one so I can&rsquo;t comment on their relative merits but they both seem like useful and worthy packages.
</p>
<p>
If you&rsquo;re bad about saving your files and worry that you may lose work, you should take a look at these packages. One or both of them may be just what you need.</p>
